  "question": {
    "text": "In a photoelectric effect experiment a light of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> times the threshold frequency is made to fall on the surface of photosensitive material. Now if the frequency is halved and intensity is doubled, the number of photo electrons emitted will be:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "UpzLL",
      "text": "Doubled",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aLU4S",
      "text": "Quadrupled",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "889p3",
      "text": "Zero",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "QpETh",
      "text": "Halved",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. Now, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> i.e. the incident frequency is less than threshold frequency. Hence, there will be no emission of photoelectrons.</p><p>Therefore, current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and no photo electrons will be emitted.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
